+> ~/.local/share/chanto-bench/work-resume-0/aider/swe-pylint-dev__pylint-5859-0/tests/functional/i/implicit/implicit_str_concat_latin1.py: 'utf-8' codec can't decode byte 0xe9 in position 105: invalid continuation byte  
+> Use --encoding to set the unicode encoding.  
+
+Looking at the issue: in `pylint/checkers/misc.py`, the `EncodingChecker.open()` method builds a regex that ends with `\b` (a word-boundary assert). A `\b` only matches at a transition between a word char (`\w`) and a non-word char (`\W`). When the note tag is entirely punctuation (e.g. `???`), the characters on both sides of the tag are non-word (`?` ... `:`/space), so no boundary exists and the tag is never matched.
+
+The fix is to replace `\b` with a lookahead `(?=\W|$)` that asserts the tag is followed by a non-word character or end-of-string, regardless of the character preceding it. This preserves the original protection against partial-word matches (e.g. `TODOx` still won't match `TODO`) while allowing punctuation-only tags to work.
+
+Relevant tests to run after the change:
+- `pytest tests/checkers/unittest_misc.py` (if present)
+- the functional tests covering `fixme`/notes (e.g. under `tests/functional/m/miscellaneous/` and any `notes`/`fixme` functional cases)
